Hmmm… Actually, I’m trying to adapt scientific computing into OOP styled python (specifically, PySCF). I knew from DRY principle in The Pragmatic Programmer (page 8 in this pdf) that we could write code like this:
class Line {
    private double length; 
    private Point start; 
    private Point end;

    public Line(Point start, Point end) {
        this.start = start; this.end = end;
        calculateLength() 
    }
    public void setStart(Point p) {
         this.start = p;
         calculate_length() 
    } 
    public void setEnd(Point p) {
        this.end = p;
        calculate_length() 
    }

Point getStart(void) { return start; } 
Point getEnd(void) { return end; }
double getLength() { return length; }

Because of this setter function, every time start and end changes, lengthwill change automatically. (I like this style.)
However, for programs with calculation as the main process, this situation is much more complicated. The most expensive part is the key step of some calculations (i.e. calc process in setEnd becomes much more expensive while setStart is relatively cheap, so we can’t automatically update length in setEnd every time a property of Point end changes. Instead, manually after all parameters have been set properly, run.) . Moreover, during the process of the expensive one, there may be some intermediate variables shared by many subroutines.
What I want to do is taking the advantage of OOP. Only the starting data and the final results matter. So I want to set the starting readable and writable, the ending readable (for further advanced calculations), while keeping those unimportant intermediate variables private.

I’m a green hand in software engineering and I don’t really know how many variables I should set public. The more I set, the more inner functions are coupled. While the less I set, the more arguments a function will have. When I write subroutines, I need to do a lot of this:
# In a class
def calc(self, A=None, B=None, C=None):
    if A is None:
        A = self.A
    if B is None:
        B = self.B
    if C is None:
        C = self.C
    D = self.D # intermediate shared by many subroutines.
    # Troublesome? I think so.
    # some code to calc using A, B, C and D
    return xxx

I write like this so that I can write a function in this class like this:
def wanna_self_consistent_convergence(self):
    while True:
        old_status_A = self.A
        old_potential = self.potential
        new_status_A = get_from(old_potential)

        new_potential = calc(new_status_A)
        if abs(new_potential - old_potential) < threshold:
             break
        self.A = new_status_A
        self.potential = new_potential

While in other cases, when others want to do a single calculation using the current properties, he could do this:
mycalc = myclass(init_vars)
mycalc.calc() # using current vars with no need to update

I want to know best practices to avoid these.

Another question I have is that I don’t really know how to declare a variable in python. I tend to do this:
class myclass:
    def __init__(self, obj_):
        # declaration
        self.varA = None
        ...
        # initialization
        self.varA = self.updateA(obj_)

But it seems not pythonic. And another question is: should I write self.varA = self.updateA(obj_) or self.updateA(obj_) in __init__ function?
Thank you!

Is it a requirement that your classes are mutable? I think there’s something to be said for not changing the start or end on existing Line objects, and instead creating a new Line. Then your Line class simply becomes this:
class Line:
    def __init__(self, start, end):
        self.start = start
        self.end = end
        self.length = calculate_length(start, end)

    def replace_start(self, new_start):
        return Line(new_start, self.length)

    def replace_end(self, new_end):
        return Line(self.start, new_end)

Each Line instance has a length that’s calculated once at initialization. Use replace_start() or replace_end() to create a new instance with another start or end, and with a new automatically calculated length. You don’t have to worry about updating the length when updating start or end of an existing instance, because that doesn’t happen anymore.
You could use a dataclass to help make the instances immutable:
import dataclasses
import math

@dataclasses.dataclass
class Point:
    x: float
    y: float

def calculate_length(a: Point, b: Point) -> float:
    return math.hypot(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y)

@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Line:
    start: Point
    end: Point
    length: float = dataclasses.field(init=False)

    def __post_init__(self): 
        # Unfortunately this gets a bit ugly to be able to set length even
        # with frozen=True
        object.__setattr__(self, "length", calculate_length(self.start, self.end))

    def replace_start(self, new_start: Point) -> Line:
        return Line(new_start, self.end)

    def replace_end(self, new_end: Point) -> Line:
        return Line(self.start, new_end)

def main():
    a = Line(Point(0, 0), Point(3, 4))
    print(a)
    b = a.replace_start(Point(1, 1))
    print(b)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()

That would print:
