I’d say, we cannot re calc immediately after num_of_electrons change, instead, after all variables are set, do.


Is there a reason that you need to calculate it at all until the user needs it? Rather than calculating proactively, you could do something like this:
class Mole:
    def __init__(self):
        self._atom = ""
        self._num_electrons = None

    @property
    def atom(self):
        return self._atom

    @atom.setter
    def atom(self, value):
        self._atom = value
        self._num_electrons = None

    @property
    def num_electrons(self):
        if self._num_electrons is None:
            # recalculate here
            self._num_electrons = 1
        return self._num_electrons

It still feels like @duncathan’s example fits very well here. I don’t see where you’re seeing “to invoke all of the calculating functions manually”. I would extend that code with an ability to force expensive calculation now; and maybe a generic “dirty” flag instead of per-field state; I did something roughly similar in some projects in the past:
class Mole:
    def __init__(self, atom):
        self._atom = atom
        self._mark_dirty()

    def _mark_dirty(self):
        self._dirty = True
        self._num_electrons = None
        self._num_neutrons = None
        self._num_protons = None

    # called whenever you access a field that needs recalculation,
    # but you can also call it manually if you want to force the long calculation to happen _now_
    def recalculate(self):
        if not self._dirty:
            return
        a, b, c = some_expensive_calculation_here(self._atom)
        self._num_electrons = a
        self._num_neutrons = b
        self._num_protons = c
        self._dirty = False

    @property
    def atom(self):
        return self._atom

    @atom.setter
    def atom(self, value):
        self._atom = value
        # optional enchancement: only _mark_dirty() if ._atom actually changed
        self._mark_dirty()

    @property
    def num_electrons(self): # identical for num_neutrons, num_protons
        self.recalculate()
        return self._num_electrons
